    {
      "id": "q521zmp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Factors Affecting the Climate of India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "India's climate is driven by factors such as latitude, altitude, relief features, and surrounding ocean bodies.",
        "The Indian monsoon operates through complex thermal contrasts between land mass and oceans, alongside jet streams.",
        "The Southwest Monsoon splits into two primary branches: the Arabian Sea Branch and the Bay of Bengal Branch.",
        "El Niño generally weakens the Indian monsoon leading to droughts, whereas La Niña enhances rainfall.",
        "Rainfall distribution in India is highly uneven, ranging from heavy rainfall in the Western Ghats to arid conditions in Rajasthan."
      ],
      "id": "neiqsa9"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7vp404i",
      "content": "India’s climate is influenced by its latitudinal position, relief, altitude, distance from sea, pressure systems and jet streams. These factors operate together to produce extreme regional diversity in temperature and rainfall."
    },
